file: "packages/editor/src/shell/context_menu.tsx"
doc: "The right-click menu: a positioned list of commands, dispatching on choose."

structs: [
  { name: "ContextMenuProps"
    doc:
      "`items` are the rows, already filtered for the current selection. The\n"
      "component does no filtering of its own and holds no selection.\n"
      "\n"
      "`at` is where the POINTER WAS, in CLIENT coordinates -- the same space\n"
      "as PointerEvent.clientX/clientY -- not diagram coordinates, and not\n"
      "necessarily where the menu\'s corner ends up. The component derives\n"
      "the corner from it by clamping, below. The menu is chrome positioned\n"
      "over the viewport and never pans or zooms with the diagram.\n"
      "\n"
      "`onChoose` receives the chosen command id. The component does NOT run\n"
      "it: it has no CommandContext, and giving it one would put a second\n"
      "dispatcher beside the registry, which is the same reason\n"
      "CommandPalette takes onChoose rather than running commands itself.\n"
      "\n"
      "`onClose` is dismissal WITHOUT a choice: an outside click, Escape, a\n"
      "scroll or a resize. Choosing a row calls onChoose and NOT onClose --\n"
      "the component never calls both for one interaction. Closing is the\n"
      "caller\'s job either way; see App, which routes both paths through a\n"
      "single close function so there is one line that closes the menu."
    fields: [
      { name: "items",    type: { named: { symbol: "ReadonlyArray", args: [{ named: { symbol: "MenuItem" } }] } } },
      { name: "at",       type: { named: { symbol: "Vec2" } } },
      { name: "onChoose", type: { arrow: { inputs: [{ named: { symbol: "CommandId" } }], output: { primitive: VOID } } } },
      { name: "onClose",  type: { arrow: { output: { primitive: VOID } } } }
    ]
    typescript: { struct_kind: STRUCT_KIND_INTERFACE }
  }
]

constants: [
  { name: "ContextMenu"
    doc:
      "Renders `items` as a list at `at`, styled from the same island tokens\n"
      "the palette uses -- --ag-panel, --ag-edge, --ag-radius, --ag-shadow --\n"
      "so it reads as the same family of surface.\n"
      "\n"
      "KEYBOARD, and it owns these keys while open:\n"
      "  ArrowDown / ArrowUp  move the highlight, CLAMPING at each end.\n"
      "                       They do not wrap, for the same reason the\n"
      "                       palette's do not: a wrap makes a held key\n"
      "                       cycle silently.\n"
      "  Enter                choose the highlighted row.\n"
      "  Escape               onClose.\n"
      "The highlight starts on the FIRST row when the menu opens.\n"
      "\n"
      "ALL FOUR are handled on the menu\'s own element and ALL FOUR call\n"
      "stopPropagation, exactly as CommandPalette does. None of them reaches\n"
      "the document keydown listener in canvas.tsx while the menu is open.\n"
      "\n"
      "Escape is the one with a visible failure mode, and it is worth naming\n"
      "because it is not obvious: the menu is NOT on ui.overlay() -- its\n"
      "position is a local signal in App -- so the `escape` command does not\n"
      "see an open overlay and takes its second branch, clearing the\n"
      "selection. Unstopped, one Escape would close the menu AND clear the\n"
      "selection the menu was opened on. The arrows matter for the same\n"
      "reason in miniature: unstopped, they would also nudge.\n"
      "\n"
      "Clicking anywhere outside closes via onClose. So does scrolling or\n"
      "resizing -- a menu pinned to a stale client point is worse than no\n"
      "menu.\n"
      "\n"
      "EDGE CLAMPING. `at` may be close enough to the right or bottom edge\n"
      "that the menu would overflow. The corner is:\n"
      "  x = min(at.x, window.innerWidth  - menuWidth),  then floored at 0\n"
      "  y = min(at.y, window.innerHeight - menuHeight), then floored at 0\n"
      "-- innerHeight against the menu\'s HEIGHT on the y axis, not a repeat\n"
      "of the width expression.\n"
      "\n"
      "For any menu that fits in the window this puts the whole of it\n"
      "on-screen. A menu TALLER than the window cannot be fully visible, and\n"
      "the floor at 0 is what decides that case: the top-left is shown and\n"
      "the overflow falls off the bottom, rather than the reverse. That is a\n"
      "consequence of the rule, not a separate one, and no guarantee of full\n"
      "visibility is made for it.\n"
      "\n"
      "An empty `items` array renders nothing at all rather than an empty\n"
      "box, matching StateIsland's convention for a component with nothing\n"
      "to say."
    type: { named: { symbol: "Component", args: [{ named: { symbol: "ContextMenuProps" } }] } }
    uses: ["MenuItem"]
  }
]
